window.MY_APPS_BUNDLE = {"stamp": "2026-11-03 11:07", "files": {"scripts.json": "{\n  \"title\": \"My Apps — rebuilding my own offline apps\",\n  \"stages\": [\n    { \"key\": \"tutorial\", \"file\": \"tutorial.md\",  \"label\": \"Tutorial\",    \"hint\": \"How the app works, block by block. The app itself stays private: open it from Offline_apps and read it side by side.\" },\n    { \"key\": \"notes\",    \"file\": \"notes.md\",     \"label\": \"1 · Notes\",   \"hint\": \"Block by block, in my own words, plus answers to the hint questions. Claude checks these.\" },\n    { \"key\": \"rewrite\",  \"file\": \"rewrite.html\", \"label\": \"2 · Rewrite\", \"hint\": \"From a blank file, using only my notes. It can be smaller than the original.\" },\n    { \"key\": \"change\",   \"file\": \"change.html\",  \"label\": \"3 · Feature\", \"hint\": \"My rewrite plus one feature of my own.\" },\n    { \"key\": \"new\",      \"file\": \"new.html\",     \"label\": \"4 · New\",     \"hint\": \"A similar app, written from scratch.\" }\n  ],\n  \"scripts\": [\n    { \"id\": \"01_part_matrix\", \"title\": \"Part Matrix Finder\",\n      \"source\": \"Offline_apps / Mechanical/#Part_matrix/part_matrix_finder_v2.html\",\n      \"taken\": \"2026-11-03\",\n      \"concepts\": [\"one-file app\", \"CSS tokens + dark theme\", \"parse pasted Excel / CSV\", \"localStorage\", \"pure filter function\", \"event delegation\", \"CSV export with BOM\", \"print\"] },\n    { \"id\": \"02_batsmith\", \"title\": \"BatSmith\",\n      \"source\": \"Offline_apps / Coding/BatSmith/index.html\",\n      \"taken\": \"2026-11-03\",\n      \"concepts\": [\"one-file app\", \"templates as data\", \"form built from field lists\", \"syntax highlighter (RegExp)\", \"<dialog>\", \"Blob download\", \"saved library in localStorage\", \"service worker only over http\"] }\n  ]\n}\n", "log.md": "# Learning log\n\nMethod per app: (1) notes in my own words, Claude checks → (2) rewrite from blank using only notes → (3) one feature → (4) a similar new app from scratch.\nHints before answers. Claude writes no code unless I ask.\n\nPlan: Part Matrix Finder first (smaller, 649 lines), then BatSmith (read the shared parts and two templates only).\n\n| Date | App | Stage | What I learned | What confused me |\n|------|-----|-------|----------------|------------------|\n", "01_part_matrix/tutorial.md": "# Part Matrix Finder — how it works\n\nA tutorial on my own app. The app itself is **not** stored here: open it from\n`Offline_apps / Mechanical/#Part_matrix/part_matrix_finder_v2.html` (or the same path in Drive,\n`App_generation_offline`) and read it side by side. Line numbers refer to that file (649 lines, snapshot 2026-11-03).\n\n**Kind 1: one-file app.** HTML, CSS and JavaScript are all in one file. No libraries, no server.\nBootcamp lessons it uses: 15 boilerplate · 21 tables · 26 forms · 31–39 CSS · 45 box model · 109–111 strings ·\n126–129 if/else · 132–137 arrays and loops · 141–148 DOM · 163–172 events.\n\n## The big picture\n\nThe app has two screens and one data model:\n\n1. **Load screen**: paste the Excel \"Data\" sheet or drop a CSV.\n2. **Main screen**: pick spec codes (Items) and part names (Variant); a matrix shows every part marked **A** for all chosen codes.\n3. **Model**: the pasted text becomes `{ codes, parts, names, labelKeys }` once. Every filter after that works on this model, not on the text.\n\n```\npaste / drop → parseDelimited() → buildModel() → MODEL ─┬→ applyFilter() → render()\n                                                         └→ localStorage (opens ready next time)\n```\n\n## Lines 1–148 — head and CSS\n\n- Colour **tokens** on `:root` (`--paper`, `--ink`, `--items`, `--variant`…) and a second set under\n  `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]`. Every rule uses `var(--…)`, so dark mode is just the second set of tokens.\n- Comment headers split the CSS by screen part: top bar, filter box, suggestion list, matrix, load screen.\n- `@media print` at the end hides the controls, so **Print** gives a clean table.\n\n## Lines 150–224 — the two screens in HTML\n\n- `<section class=\"load\" id=\"load\">` and `<section id=\"main\" class=\"hidden\">`: both screens are in the page all\n  the time. JavaScript only swaps the `hidden` class.\n- The paste box is a `<textarea id=\"paste\">`. The file picker is a hidden `<input type=\"file\" accept=\".csv,.tsv,.txt\">`\n  behind a normal button, because a real file input can't be styled nicely.\n- Every element the script needs has an `id`. That list of ids is the contract between HTML and JavaScript.\n\n## Lines 226–266 — reading pasted text\n\n`guessDelim()` decides what separates the cells by counting in the first 4,000 characters:\n\n```js\nif (tabs > 0 && tabs >= commas && tabs >= semis) return '\\t';\nif (semis > commas) return ';';\nreturn ',';\n```\n\nExcel puts TABs on the clipboard; a CSV file uses commas (or semicolons in some countries).\n\n`parseDelimited()` reads the text **one character at a time**, because a cell like `\"Bracket, hose clamp\"` contains\na comma that is not a separator. It remembers whether it is inside quotes (`inQ`), and a doubled quote `\"\"` inside\nquotes means one real `\"`. This is a small *state machine*: the same character means different things depending on the state.\n\n## Lines 268–327 — building the model\n\n`buildModel()` turns rows into something easy to filter:\n\n- It **finds the header row** by looking for the cells \"item no\" and \"item name\" in the first 20 rows. If they're missing,\n  it falls back to the row with the most short, code-like cells.\n- Every column to the right of Qty is a **spec code**.\n- Rows whose Item No looks like `Group:` are **labels** (group headings). They are remembered and attached to the parts\n  below them. Rows with a number are **parts**.\n- `marks` stores only the cells that have something in them, keyed by column number. That keeps the model small.\n- It ends with `names` (every part name, unique and sorted, via `new Set`) for the suggestion list.\n\nWhen something is wrong it uses `throw new Error('…')`, and `load()` shows the message to the user.\n\n## Lines 329–351 — the filter\n\n`applyFilter(model, f)` is a **pure function**: it only reads the model and returns `{ parts, cols }`. It never touches the page.\n\n- A part passes only if **every** selected code is marked `A` on it (`for … if (!hit(…)) return false`).\n- Part names are OR'ed: any one of them matching is enough.\n- `cols` keeps only the code columns that are used by at least one remaining part, so the matrix shrinks as you filter.\n\nKeeping the logic pure makes it easy to test, and it is why the CSV export and the screen always agree.\n\n## Lines 353–367 — state and theme\n\n- A few global variables hold the state: `MODEL`, `sel` (the current filter), `showAll`, `LAST` (the last result).\n- `var $ = function (id) { return document.getElementById(id); };` is a short helper used everywhere.\n- The theme block runs once at start: saved choice, otherwise the computer's setting (`matchMedia`).\n\n## Lines 369–410 — loading data\n\n- `load(text, label)` runs the whole pipeline inside `try { } catch`, saves the **raw text** in `localStorage`\n  (key `pmf.data.v1`), switches screens and calls `render()`.\n- Pasting loads straight away: a `paste` listener waits 30 ms (`setTimeout`), because the text isn't in the box yet\n  at the moment the event fires.\n- `FileReader` reads a chosen or dropped file, and `dragover` / `drop` listeners on the whole `document` make the entire page a drop zone.\n\n## Lines 412–512 — tag fields with suggestions\n\n`makeField('I')` and `makeField('V')` build the two filter boxes from one function: one function, two fields.\nInside, `options()` lists suggestions, `draw()` shows them, `pick()` adds a tag, and `keydown` handles ↑ ↓, Enter,\nEscape and Backspace. The suggestion list is hidden on `blur` after a short delay, so a click on a suggestion still counts.\n\nThe × on each tag is handled by **one** listener on the whole document, using `e.target.closest('.tag button')`.\nThat is *event delegation*: tags are re-drawn all the time, so listeners on the tags themselves would be lost.\n\n## Lines 514–599 — render\n\n`render()` redraws everything from `sel` and `MODEL`:\n\n- runs `applyFilter`, updates the counts, and shows only the first 300 rows (`CAP`) unless you choose \"Show all\";\n- puts your selected code columns first;\n- prints group headings only when a group label changes between rows, and drops label levels that are the same for every row;\n- builds the whole table as one HTML string and sets `innerHTML` once. Every value goes through `esc()` first, so text from the sheet can never break the page.\n\n## Lines 601–640 — clicks on the table, export, print\n\n- One `click` listener on the table: header click toggles that code in the filter, name click adds the name as a variant.\n- A `mouseover` listener highlights the whole column under the mouse (the \"crosshair\").\n- **Export CSV** writes the current result with `csvCell()` (quotes cells containing `,` `\"` or line breaks) and a\n  `'﻿'` BOM so Excel opens Japanese text correctly. It's the same export as Mini Matrix step 5 in the workshop.\n\n## Lines 642–646 — start-up\n\nThe last block tries to load the saved text from `localStorage`. If there is none, or it no longer parses, it shows the\nload screen. It's placed last so that every function it calls already exists.\n\n## What to look for when you read it\n\n- Where the app keeps **state**, and the one function that redraws from it.\n- Why the parser walks character by character instead of using `split(',')`.\n- Which functions touch the page and which don't (`applyFilter` doesn't).\n", "01_part_matrix/notes.md": "# Part Matrix Finder — my notes\n\nStarted:\n\nOpen the app from `Offline_apps / Mechanical/#Part_matrix/part_matrix_finder_v2.html` and read it next to the tutorial.\n\n## Block by block\n\nFor each block: what it does · why it's there (what breaks without it) · what each name means.\n\n### Lines 1–148 — head and CSS (tokens, dark theme, print)\n\n### Lines 150–224 — the two screens in HTML\n\n### Lines 226–232 — clean()\n\n### Lines 234–266 — guessDelim() and parseDelimited()\n\n### Lines 268–327 — buildModel()\n\n### Lines 329–351 — applyFilter()\n\n### Lines 353–367 — state, $, esc, theme\n\n### Lines 369–410 — loading: load(), readFile(), paste, drag and drop\n\n### Lines 412–512 — tag fields: makeField(), tags(), the × buttons\n\n### Lines 514–599 — render()\n\n### Lines 601–640 — table clicks, crosshair, CSV export, print\n\n### Lines 642–646 — start-up\n\n## Hint questions\n\n1. Line 228: what is `_x000D_`, and where does it come from? (Hint: copy a cell with a line break in Excel.)\n2. Lines 235–241: paste a CSV that uses `;` with a few commas inside the cells. Which separator wins, and why?\n3. Lines 249–264: follow the text `a,\"b,c\",d` character by character. What are `inQ`, `field` and `row` after each character?\n4. Lines 271–284: what happens if the sheet has no \"Item No\" header? Which row becomes the header?\n5. Lines 305–312: what is a \"label\" row, and how do the labels end up attached to each part?\n6. Line 331: what does the \"Also show D, * and numbered marks\" checkbox change in `applyFilter`?\n7. Line 380: why does it save the raw text instead of `MODEL`?\n8. Line 398: remove the `setTimeout`. What goes wrong when you paste?\n9. Lines 506–508: why is the × click handled on `document` instead of on each button?\n10. Line 526: what is `CAP`, and what would happen with 5,000 parts and no cap?\n\n## Experiments I ran\n\n## Still unsure about\n\n## Claude's check\n\n_(empty until reviewed)_\n", "02_batsmith/tutorial.md": "# BatSmith — how it works\n\nA tutorial on my own app. The app itself is **not** stored here: open it from\n`Offline_apps / Coding/BatSmith/index.html` (or Drive, `App_generation_offline/Coding/Batsmith/index.html`) and read\nit side by side. Line numbers refer to that file (1,947 lines, snapshot 2026-11-03).\n\n**Kind 1: one-file app** (it also installs as an app when served over http, which makes it kind 4 too).\nBootcamp lessons it uses: 26 forms · 45–50 layout · 109–113 strings · 132–137 arrays and loops · 141–148 DOM ·\n163–172 events · 167–169 objects and methods · 168 switch.\n\n## The big idea: templates are data\n\nBatSmith never contains 28 finished `.bat` files. It has **28 template objects** in one array `T`, and each one knows how\nto *build* its script from the options you pick. Change an option and the script is rebuilt from scratch.\n\n```\npick a type (T[i]) → its fields become a form → your values (o) → T[i].build(o) → lines → highlighted preview\n                                                                                       └→ download .bat / copy\n```\n\n## Lines 1–172 — head and CSS\n\n- Colour tokens with three themes: **auto** (follows the computer), **light** and **dark**.\n- Classes `tk-k`, `tk-v`, `tk-s`, `tk-o`, `tk-c`, `tk-l` colour the preview: keywords, variables, strings,\n  operators, comments, labels.\n\n## Lines 174–252 — the screen\n\n- `<header class=\"appbar\">`: buttons for the command index (`#idxBtn`), the saved library (`#libBtn`) and the theme (`#themeBtn`).\n- `<main>`: a search box (`#q`) and the list of types (`#tlist`), the form (`#form`) with the file name, and the preview\n  (`#code`) with Copy, Save, Download and \"Explain every line\".\n- Two `<dialog>` elements: the **command index** and **saved scripts**. A `<dialog>` is a built-in pop-up:\n  `showModal()` opens it, `close()` closes it, and Esc works for free.\n\n## Lines 258–283 — helpers: one line = `{ c, w }`\n\nEvery generated line is an object: `c` is the **code**, `w` is the **why** (the explanation shown when you click the line).\n\n```js\nvar L = function (c, w) { return { c: c, w: w || \"\" }; };\n```\n\nSmall helpers build common pieces: `rem()` adds a comment if comments are on, `echo()` and `et()` make text safe for\n`echo` by escaping the characters cmd would act on (`^ & | < > ( ) %`), `block()` indents the body of an `if ( … )` block,\nand `fail()` writes the standard \"print an error, pause, exit /b 1\" lines.\n\n## Lines 285–392 — shared fields and pieces\n\nMany templates need the same options (which folder, window title, what happens at the end, git remote and branch).\nThey are written once, as functions that return **field lists**: `FOLDER()`, `COMMON()`, `GIT_FIELDS`. Each field is\nplain data:\n\n```js\n{ k: \"title\", label: \"Window title\", type: \"text\", def: title }\n```\n\n`k` is the key in your values, `type` says which control to draw, `def` is the default, and an optional\n`show: function (o) { … }` hides the field when it doesn't apply.\n\n`head()` writes `@echo off`, the title and `setlocal`. `findPython()` writes the lines that look for `py -3` or `python`.\n\n## Lines 394–1671 — the 28 templates\n\nEach `T.push({ … })` is one type. The first one, **Git pull** (lines 397–420), shows the full shape:\n\n- `id`, `name`, `cat` (category), `file` (default file name), `blurb`, `concepts` (the batch ideas it teaches);\n- `fields`: the form, assembled from the shared pieces plus its own options;\n- `build(o)`: returns the list of lines, written with `head()`, `rem()`, `L()`, `block()` and so on;\n- `warn(o)`: notes shown above the preview (for example \"Test git scripts in a throwaway repo first\").\n\nThe rest follow the same shape: files and text (backup, clean up, find text, sort…), loops, menus and small games,\nlaunchers, system and network tools.\n\n## Lines 1673–1683 — state\n\nAll your choices live in one object `S`, saved under `localStorage` key `batsmith_v1`: the chosen type (`S.tpl`),\nthe values per type (`S.vals`), file names and the saved library. `vals(t)` merges your saved values with each\nfield's default, so a new field in a later version still gets a value.\n\n## Lines 1685–1711 — the syntax highlighter\n\nThree regular expressions (`RX_FULL`, `RX_TEXT`, `RX_STR`) find strings, `%variables%`, keywords and operators.\n`paint()` walks through the matches and wraps each in a `<span class=\"tk-…\">`, escaping everything in between.\n`hl(line)` handles whole-line cases first (comments, labels, `echo` text), then calls `paint()`.\n\n## Lines 1713–1784 — drawing the screen\n\n- `gen(t, o)` calls `t.build(o)` and, if any line has non-English characters, inserts `chcp 65001` so cmd reads\n  the file as UTF-8.\n- `renderList()` groups the types by category and filters them by the search text.\n- `renderForm()` turns the field list into HTML: a checkbox, a `<select>`, a `<textarea>` or an `<input>` per field.\n  **The form is not written in the HTML at all**: it's generated from data.\n- `renderOut()` builds the script, numbers the lines, highlights them and adds the explanation under each line.\n- `all()` = list + form + preview.\n\n## Lines 1786–1828 — files and the library\n\n`download()` makes a `Blob`, a temporary URL and an `<a download>` and clicks it, the same pattern as the workshop's\nexport (lesson 5.2). Line ends are `\\r\\n`, because Windows batch files expect them. **Save** stores the current type,\nvalues and file name in `S.lib`.\n\n## Lines 1830–1897 — the command index\n\n`buildIndex()` **generates every template's script** (with default options, plus a few extra option sets) and searches\nthe text for each command (`robocopy`, `for /f`, `choice`…). So the index can never claim a type uses a command it\ndoesn't actually write.\n\n## Lines 1898–1944 — events and start-up\n\n- One `input` and one `change` listener on the whole form call `onField()`, which reads the changed field's id\n  (`f_title` → key `title`), saves the value and redraws only the preview. That is event delegation again.\n- A click on a preview line toggles its explanation; the \"Explain every line\" box shows them all.\n- The service worker is registered **only** over `http(s)`, so double-clicking the file still works.\n- Everything sits inside `(function () { … })();` so its many names (`T`, `S`, `L`…) don't leak into the page.\n\n## What to look for when you read it\n\n- How one data shape (`T.push({ fields, build })`) replaces 28 separate pages.\n- Where a value flows: form → `onField` → `S.vals` → `vals()` → `build(o)` → preview.\n- How the explanation travels with each line (`{ c, w }`) instead of living in a separate file.\n", "02_batsmith/notes.md": "# BatSmith — my notes\n\nStarted:\n\nOpen the app from `Offline_apps / Coding/BatSmith/index.html` and read it next to the tutorial.\nIt is long (1,947 lines): read the shared parts first, then just **two** templates (Git pull, and one you use).\n\n## Block by block\n\nFor each block: what it does · why it's there (what breaks without it) · what each name means.\n\n### Lines 1–172 — head, theme tokens, highlighter colours\n\n### Lines 174–252 — the screen: appbar, list, form, preview, two dialogs\n\n### Lines 258–283 — helpers: L, B, rem, et, echo, nq, block, fail\n\n### Lines 285–392 — shared fields: FOLDER, COMMON, head, GIT_FIELDS, findPython\n\n### Lines 397–420 — template 1: Git pull\n\n### Lines ___–___ — one more template of my choice: ______\n\n### Lines 1673–1683 — state: S, save, tpl, vals\n\n### Lines 1685–1711 — the highlighter: RX_FULL, paint, hl\n\n### Lines 1713–1784 — gen, renderList, renderForm, renderOut\n\n### Lines 1786–1828 — download and the saved library\n\n### Lines 1830–1897 — the command index\n\n### Lines 1898–1944 — events and start-up\n\n## Hint questions\n\n1. Line 259: why is each line an object `{ c, w }` and not just a string? What would be harder without `w`?\n2. Lines 263–267: what does `et(\"50% & more\")` return, and why?\n3. Lines 272–277: what does `block()` do to the lines you give it?\n4. Lines 302–311: why are `COMMON()` and `FOLDER()` functions that *return* field lists, instead of one shared array?\n5. Lines 397–420: change \"Rebase instead of merge\" in the app. Which line of `build()` reacts to it?\n6. Lines 1679–1683: you add a new field with a `def` to a template. Old saved values don't have it. What does `vals()` do?\n7. Lines 1718–1723: type a Japanese folder name in any template. What line appears, and where is it inserted?\n8. Lines 1744–1765: where in the HTML file is the form written? (Search for `id=\"form\"`.) How do its controls get there?\n9. Lines 1914–1919: how does `onField` know which key to save when you type in a box?\n10. Lines 1867–1881: why build every script to make the index, instead of typing the list of commands by hand?\n11. Line 1941: open the app by double-click and through a local server. When is `sw.js` registered?\n\n## Experiments I ran\n\n## Still unsure about\n\n## Claude's check\n\n_(empty until reviewed)_\n"}};
